        'Every legal system must eventually confront a deceptively simple question: when the words of a law appear to produce an absurd or unjust result, should judges follow the words or the purpose behind them? The question is ancient, but it acquires fresh urgency whenever lawmakers draft in haste or technology outpaces the language of statutes.',
        'A celebrated illustration is the hypothetical rule prohibiting "vehicles in the park". Does it apply to a bicycle? A child\'s electric toy car? An ambulance responding to an emergency? A military tank mounted as a war memorial? Read literally, the rule seems to ban all of these; construed in light of its evident purpose — presumably to protect pedestrians and preserve tranquillity — it bans only some. Textualists argue that judges who look beyond the letter of the law usurp the role of elected legislators, substituting their own preferences for the words that were actually enacted. Purposivists reply that language is inevitably ambiguous and that a court which applies words without regard to their purpose may produce outcomes no legislator could ever have intended.',
        'In practice, most courts adopt neither position in its pure form. They begin with the ordinary meaning of the text, depart from it only insofar as it would produce manifest absurdity, and consult legislative history, precedent and the broader statutory scheme where ambiguity remains. This compromise is, admittedly, untidy. Notwithstanding its untidiness, it reflects a truth that lawyers and linguists share: words do not interpret themselves. Every reading is an act of judgement — and the legitimacy of the law depends, in no small part, on whether that judgement is exercised transparently.',
