      model: 'The principle that judges should apply the law exactly as written has considerable appeal. It promises predictability, restrains judicial power and respects the democratic legitimacy of legislators, who alone are entitled to make law. Notwithstanding these virtues, a strictly literal approach is, in my view, neither possible nor desirable as an absolute rule. It is not possible because language is inherently ambiguous. Even an apparently simple prohibition, such as one forbidding "vehicles in the park", cannot be applied without deciding what counts as a vehicle, a decision the text itself does not make. Every application of a rule therefore involves interpretation, whether or not judges acknowledge it. Nor is strict literalism desirable. Statutes are drafted by human beings who cannot anticipate every situation. A rule whereby emergency vehicles were excluded from a park, for instance, would produce results that no legislator could plausibly have intended. A court that enforced such a rule regardless of the consequences would be faithful to the letter of the law while betraying its purpose. This is not to say that judges should be free to substitute their own preferences for those of parliament. The most defensible approach, albeit an untidy one, begins with the ordinary meaning of the text and departs from it only insofar as that meaning would produce manifest absurdity. Such departures should be reasoned openly and grounded in precedent and legislative purpose. In conclusion, judges should be bound by the law, but not imprisoned by its wording. Transparent interpretation, rather than mechanical application, is what ultimately sustains public confidence in the courts.',
