      model: 'Gone are the days when "machine" meant a lump of metal and "human" a creature of flesh and blood. Already, millions of people live with pacemakers, cochlear implants and insulin pumps; already, our memories are outsourced to phones and our decisions shaped by algorithms. It is tempting to conclude that, by 2100, the line between human and machine will have dissolved completely. Such a conclusion, however, confuses integration with identity. That we increasingly augment our bodies with technology does not mean we become indistinguishable from it. A person with a prosthetic leg is no less a person; a cyborg, in the literal sense, remains a human being who uses tools of extraordinary intimacy. What would genuinely blur the distinction is not the presence of machines in humans, but the presence of something like experience in machines. So uncertain is our understanding of consciousness that we cannot yet say whether a sufficiently complex system could be sentient. Behind every confident prediction in this area lies, at best, informed conjecture. The more pressing question, therefore, may be political rather than metaphysical. If enhancement becomes available only to some, then by 2100 the most meaningful distinction may not be between humans and machines, but between augmented and unaugmented humans. So serious would the consequences of that divide be that it deserves our attention now, long before any machine claims to feel. In short, the distinction will remain meaningful — but what it means will depend on choices we have not yet made.',
