      model: 'I believe it\'s entirely possible to live meaningfully without an ultimate purpose. In fact, the absence of a given purpose may make our choices more significant, not less. Of course, people who lose religious certainty are liable to feel a kind of void at first, and some will be tempted by nihilism. But nihilism makes a logical leap: from "nothing has ultimate meaning" to "nothing matters", which doesn\'t follow. If meaning is created rather than discovered, then it is incumbent upon us to create it responsibly — through relationships, work and care for others. Would that it were easy; it isn\'t. But it may be more authentic.',
