        'Listen to any financial news bulletin and you will hear a strange kind of meteorology. Markets are "nervous" or "buoyant"; storms "gather" over the eurozone; confidence "evaporates"; an economy "overheats" and must be "cooled". The language is so familiar that it barely registers as metaphor at all. Yet linguists who study economic discourse argue that these images do more than decorate: they quietly shape what we believe economies are and what can be done about them.',
        'Consider the dominant metaphors. When markets are described as weather or as living organisms with moods, economic outcomes appear natural and largely beyond human control — something to be forecast and endured rather than decided. When the economy is a body, crises become illnesses: contagion spreads, ailing firms need rescue, recoveries are "fragile". When it is a machine, policymakers become engineers adjusting levers, aiming for a "soft landing". Each framing highlights certain responses and obscures others. The weather metaphor, in particular, tends to conceal agency: nobody is responsible for a storm.',
