      model: 'Economic reporting relies heavily on metaphor. Markets panic and rally, economies overheat and stall, and crises spread through financial systems like disease. Such language is often defended as a necessary simplification of complex processes. Nevertheless, there are good reasons to believe that it conceals at least as much as it explains. Metaphors are valuable precisely because they make the abstract tangible. Describing the 2008 crisis as financial contagion captured something real about how losses in one market triggered panic in others. Similarly, the image of a central bank aiming for a soft landing conveys the delicate balance between controlling inflation and avoiding recession more vividly than any equation could. However, metaphors also carry assumptions. When markets are described as jittery or confident, they are cast as autonomous creatures with moods, rather than as the aggregated decisions of identifiable institutions. Weather metaphors — storms gathering, confidence evaporating — go further, implying that economic outcomes are natural events beyond anyone\'s control. Such framings tend to obscure questions of responsibility: no one is blamed for a hurricane. Even medical metaphors are not neutral. Describing a struggling firm as ailing and in need of rescue invites public money to prop it up, while describing the same firm as reckless and over-leveraged would invite a very different response. In conclusion, economic metaphors are indispensable, but they are never innocent. The critical reader should neither reject them nor accept them uncritically, but ask, in each case, which actors and choices the image allows us to forget.',
