      model: 'It is often argued that every word carries a perspective, and that the very notion of neutral reporting is therefore an illusion. If no description can be entirely free of evaluation, the argument goes, then all journalism is, in effect, propaganda. This position contains an important insight, but its conclusion is, in my view, both inaccurate and dangerous. The insight is that word choice is never innocent. Whether a group is described as freedom fighters, rebels or terrorists shapes how audiences judge its actions before any facts are presented. Euphemisms such as "collateral damage" soften violence, while dysphemisms such as "swarms of migrants" dehumanise people. Grammar, too, carries ideology: agentless passives ("shots were fired") and nominalisations ("the clearance of the area") allow responsibility to disappear. In this sense, perfect neutrality is indeed unattainable. However, it does not follow that all reporting is propaganda. Propaganda involves a deliberate attempt to manipulate, often through disinformation, selective omission and the demonisation of opponents. Responsible journalism, by contrast, attempts to minimise distortion: it names agents, quantifies claims, separates fact from comment and corrects its errors. Compare "Police clear protest camp" with "Police removed about two hundred protesters from the square on Tuesday morning". Neither is perfectly neutral, but the second is clearly more precise and more accountable. To claim that all reporting is equally propagandistic is to erase this crucial distinction — and, paradoxically, to serve the interests of genuine propagandists, who benefit when audiences believe that nothing can be trusted. In conclusion, neutrality may be an ideal rather than an achievable state, but precision and honesty remain meaningful standards by which reporting can and should be judged.',
