        'Literary writers deliberately break the rules of standard prose. Fragments (sentences without a main verb) create immediacy or emphasis: "Rain. Again. The same grey street." Long, accumulating sentences with commas and "and" can imitate the flow of thought.',
        'Free indirect speech blends narrator and character: the grammar is third person and past tense, but the thoughts, questions and exclamations are the character\'s own. Direct: She thought, "Why did I come here?" → Free indirect: Why had she come here? It was absurd.',
